%S A402462 313,15481,42961,353641,1077001,1874641,3987001,9805321,14200321,
%T A402462 32784001,44265841,71505241,71040841,125788321,176179441,277813321,
%U A402462 306226441,529396201,872590681,707943121,1141967401,1710978361,1529923201,1718072401,3417721921,3283488841
%N A402462 Half the sum of the fourth powers of the hypotenuse and the difference of the legs: a(n) = (p^4 + (u-v)^4)/2, where u and v are the legs of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p = A002144(n).
%C A402462 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then the odd leg is u = x^2 - y^2 and the even leg is v = 2*x*y. Let h = |u-v|, so a(n) = (p^4+h^4)/2. Either leg may be the longer one. Since p and h are odd, a(n) is an integer.
%C A402462 Terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of a(n); for example, a(12) = 71505241 > a(13) = 71040841.
%C A402462 A402461 lists the hypotenuses p for which a(n) is prime. These prime values are half-quartan primes (A002646). Every prime factor of a(n) is congruent to 1 modulo 8 (A007519); see the linked note.
%C A402462 For p = A002144(n), let Q = p*a(n). The construction in A402463 gives a 3 X 3 magic square with central entry Q^2 and nine distinct integer entries, at least six of them squares.
%H A402462 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402462/b402462.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A402462 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402463/a402463.txt">Half-sums of fourth powers using the difference of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A402462 If p = A002144(n) and u, v are the legs of its primitive Pythagorean triangle, then a(n) = (p^4+(u-v)^4)/2 = p^4 - 2*p^2*u*v + 2*u^2*v^2 = (p^2-u*v)^2 + (u*v)^2.
%F A402462 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then a(n) = ((x^2+y^2)^4 + (x^2-2*x*y-y^2)^4)/2.
%F A402462 a(n) = (A002144(n)^4 + (A002365(n)-A002366(n))^4)/2.
%F A402462 Here A002365(n) and A002366(n) are the longer and shorter legs, respectively.
%F A402462 a(n) = 4*A002144(n)^4 - 2*A400364(n) - A402007(n).
%e A402462 The first Pythagorean prime is 5, whose primitive triangle has legs 3 and 4. Thus a(1) = (5^4+1^4)/2 = 313.
%e A402462 The next two hypotenuses are 13 and 17, with legs (5, 12) and (8, 15), respectively. Consequently, a(2) = (13^4+7^4)/2 = 15481 = 113*137 and a(3) = (17^4+7^4)/2 = 42961.
%e A402462 For p = 29, a(4) = (29^4+1^4)/2 = 353641.
%o A402462 (Python)
%o A402462 from math import isqrt
%o A402462 from sympy import primerange
%o A402462 def a402462(limit=270):
%o A402462     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A402462     out = []
%o A402462     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A402462         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A402462             continue
%o A402462         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A402462             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A402462             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A402462                 h = abs(x*x - y*y - 2*x*y)
%o A402462                 out.append((p**4 + h**4) // 2)
%o A402462                 break
%o A402462     return out
%Y A402462 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A002646, A007519, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445, A401446, A401740, A401741, A401742, A402006, A402007, A402008, A402461, A402463.
